WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:02.790
- In our very first
episode, we recommended

00:00:02.790 --> 00:00:04.830
buying a used car with cash

00:00:04.830 --> 00:00:07.230
as the most financially sensible option.

00:00:07.230 --> 00:00:10.140
- But for many people,
that's just not possible.

00:00:10.140 --> 00:00:12.600
The majority of Americans
finance their vehicles

00:00:12.600 --> 00:00:16.020
with loans, and with rising
prices and rising interest rates

00:00:16.020 --> 00:00:18.750
car loans are getting
more expensive than ever.

00:00:18.750 --> 00:00:22.010
- [Julia] The average interest
rate on a new car jumped

00:00:22.010 --> 00:00:25.800
from 4.4% to 7% in just the last year.

00:00:25.800 --> 00:00:29.130
The amount that Americans
now owe on cars has doubled

00:00:29.130 --> 00:00:34.130
since 2011, making up over
9% of total US consumer debt.

00:00:34.560 --> 00:00:36.420
- You'd think that this
might be a good time

00:00:36.420 --> 00:00:39.570
to forego buying and lease a car instead.

00:00:39.570 --> 00:00:40.950
No debt, no commitment,

00:00:40.950 --> 00:00:43.380
and lower monthly
payments than a car loan.

00:00:43.380 --> 00:00:45.540
- They can seem attractive at first,

00:00:45.540 --> 00:00:48.870
but lease agreements have
plenty of financial pitfalls,

00:00:48.870 --> 00:00:50.220
and are just as vulnerable

00:00:50.220 --> 00:00:52.710
to price and interest
rate hikes as buying.

00:00:52.710 --> 00:00:54.750
- They're not always a bad idea.

00:00:54.750 --> 00:00:57.270
Ultimately, it depends
on what matters to you,

00:00:57.270 --> 00:01:00.374
and how much you're willing to pay for it.

00:01:00.374 --> 00:01:02.957
(jaunty music)

00:01:08.250 --> 00:01:11.040
- The fundamental
misconception about car leases

00:01:11.040 --> 00:01:13.650
is that they work like
other rental agreements,

00:01:13.650 --> 00:01:16.080
like for apartments or furniture.

00:01:16.080 --> 00:01:17.220
They don't.

00:01:17.220 --> 00:01:20.880
Cars are a unique asset
for one ugly reason:

00:01:20.880 --> 00:01:25.880
Depreciation. Nothing costs so
much yet loses value so fast

00:01:26.130 --> 00:01:29.340
as a car, except maybe
timeshares or bitcoins,

00:01:29.340 --> 00:01:32.670
and lease agreements are
designed to account for that.

00:01:32.670 --> 00:01:33.960
- Here's how it works.

00:01:33.960 --> 00:01:36.630
If you wanted to lease a
new car for the typical term

00:01:36.630 --> 00:01:39.660
of 36 months, the dealer
will take the selling price

00:01:39.660 --> 00:01:42.810
of the vehicle and subtract
what it's predicted to be worth

00:01:42.810 --> 00:01:46.050
in three years, known as the residual.

00:01:46.050 --> 00:01:48.750
The result is essentially
the depreciation,

00:01:48.750 --> 00:01:50.640
how much value the car will lose

00:01:50.640 --> 00:01:52.170
while it's in your possession,

00:01:52.170 --> 00:01:54.810
which you are required to pay
off in monthly installments.

00:01:54.810 --> 00:01:56.370
But here's the kicker.

00:01:56.370 --> 00:01:58.620
That amount is treated like a debt.

00:01:58.620 --> 00:02:01.290
Just like a car loan,
you'll pay interest and fees

00:02:01.290 --> 00:02:02.700
determined by your credit score,

00:02:02.700 --> 00:02:05.610
and often be required to
put up a down payment.

00:02:05.610 --> 00:02:08.490
- In this way, a car
lease is less of a rental,

00:02:08.490 --> 00:02:10.945
and more like buying
the most expensive years

00:02:10.945 --> 00:02:12.240
of a car's life.

00:02:12.240 --> 00:02:15.030
And because they depreciate so quickly,

00:02:15.030 --> 00:02:18.120
that can be close to half its total value.

00:02:18.120 --> 00:02:20.520
True, it's less than the sticker price.

00:02:20.520 --> 00:02:22.500
So your monthly payments are lower

00:02:22.500 --> 00:02:25.350
than a car loan would be
for an equivalent vehicle,

00:02:25.350 --> 00:02:28.890
but when the lease is up, you
have no asset to show for it.

00:02:28.890 --> 00:02:31.200
- Some lease agreements
allow you to buy the car

00:02:31.200 --> 00:02:33.600
at the end of the term
at its residual price,

00:02:33.600 --> 00:02:37.020
but because leases tend to have
higher fees than car loans,

00:02:37.020 --> 00:02:39.000
you'll end up paying more than if you had

00:02:39.000 --> 00:02:41.160
just bought it outright to begin with.

00:02:41.160 --> 00:02:44.940
- Another supposed advantage
of leasing a car is the freedom

00:02:44.940 --> 00:02:47.970
it affords. For those who
are scared of commitment,

00:02:47.970 --> 00:02:51.990
it can seem exciting to swap
vehicles every few years,

00:02:51.990 --> 00:02:55.140
but leasing comes with its
own set of restrictions.

00:02:55.140 --> 00:02:58.020
- First of all, most lease
agreements have a mileage limit

00:02:58.020 --> 00:02:59.010
for the term of the lease,

00:02:59.010 --> 00:03:02.820
usually between 12,000
and 15,000 miles per year.

00:03:02.820 --> 00:03:04.980
And if you go over that, you'll pay extra

00:03:04.980 --> 00:03:08.250
at a rate as high as 50 cents per mile.

00:03:08.250 --> 00:03:10.500
You can opt for a higher mileage limit,

00:03:10.500 --> 00:03:12.390
for a higher monthly payment of course,

00:03:12.390 --> 00:03:15.870
and you will not be reimbursed
for any miles you didn't use.

00:03:15.870 --> 00:03:17.970
- Second, you must return the vehicle

00:03:17.970 --> 00:03:20.100
exactly how you received it.

00:03:20.100 --> 00:03:22.260
So don't bother upgrading the stereo,

00:03:22.260 --> 00:03:23.970
or installing custom rims,

00:03:23.970 --> 00:03:26.520
unless you don't mind
reinstalling the old ones

00:03:26.520 --> 00:03:27.810
before you bring it back.

00:03:27.810 --> 00:03:30.720
- And lastly, lease
agreements are notoriously

00:03:30.720 --> 00:03:32.100
hard to get out of.

00:03:32.100 --> 00:03:34.830
Early termination incurs
penalties that are sometimes

00:03:34.830 --> 00:03:38.340
as much as the total
remaining balance all at once.

00:03:38.340 --> 00:03:41.340
And unlike car owners, you
can't just sell the vehicle

00:03:41.340 --> 00:03:42.750
if you don't want it anymore.

00:03:42.750 --> 00:03:45.720
You're pretty much stuck with
it until the term is over.

00:03:45.720 --> 00:03:48.930
- To be fair, there are some
advantages to leasing a car.

00:03:48.930 --> 00:03:52.080
Many lease agreements include
routine maintenance costs,

00:03:52.080 --> 00:03:54.120
and newer cars are typically still

00:03:54.120 --> 00:03:56.460
under a manufacturer's warranty.

00:03:56.460 --> 00:03:59.093
That's no small thing,
considering car repairs

00:03:59.093 --> 00:04:02.550
are one of the most common
unexpected financial crises

00:04:02.550 --> 00:04:04.050
people have to deal with.

00:04:04.050 --> 00:04:06.690
You'll never have to go through
the hassle of selling it.

00:04:06.690 --> 00:04:08.940
And if you own a small business,

00:04:08.940 --> 00:04:12.300
leasing a car can offer big tax benefits.

00:04:12.300 --> 00:04:15.960
Unlike car loans, in which you
can only deduct the interest,

00:04:15.960 --> 00:04:19.350
lease payments by businesses
are fully tax deductible.

00:04:19.350 --> 00:04:22.800
- But the main appeal of leasing
is largely psychological:

00:04:22.800 --> 00:04:25.530
having access to a nice, new, reliable car

00:04:25.530 --> 00:04:27.990
that you otherwise
wouldn't be able to afford.

00:04:27.990 --> 00:04:30.540
The benefits of comfort,
status, and safety

00:04:30.540 --> 00:04:32.040
may not be financial,

00:04:32.040 --> 00:04:33.750
but that doesn't mean they're worthless.

00:04:33.750 --> 00:04:36.510
What's the point of money
if it can't make us happy?

00:04:36.510 --> 00:04:39.870
- But are they worth enough
to overcome the cost?

00:04:39.870 --> 00:04:41.372
- I think it's time to-

00:04:41.372 --> 00:04:43.502
(together) run the numbers!

00:04:43.502 --> 00:04:46.800
(triumphant music)

00:04:46.800 --> 00:04:49.650
- [Julia] Michael and Ali are
both in the market for a car,

00:04:49.650 --> 00:04:51.570
and each has room in
their budget for around

00:04:51.570 --> 00:04:55.380
500 to 600 bucks a month, with
the same good credit score.

00:04:55.380 --> 00:04:58.800
Ali decides to buy a used car for $20,000.

00:04:58.800 --> 00:05:01.230
He puts $3,000 down, and ends up

00:05:01.230 --> 00:05:05.040
with 36 monthly payments
of about 540 bucks.

00:05:05.040 --> 00:05:07.830
- [Phillip] Michael wants
something fancier, so he decides

00:05:07.830 --> 00:05:11.400
to lease a new car with a
selling price of $40,000,

00:05:11.400 --> 00:05:13.740
which will lose about 40% of its value

00:05:13.740 --> 00:05:15.390
over the three year term.

00:05:15.390 --> 00:05:19.620
He also puts down $3,000, and
including interest and fees,

00:05:19.620 --> 00:05:24.510
he'll end up paying about
$580 a month for 36 months.

00:05:24.510 --> 00:05:26.520
- [Julia] At the end of three years,

00:05:26.520 --> 00:05:29.640
both men have paid roughly
the same amount of money,

00:05:29.640 --> 00:05:33.060
but their financial situation
couldn't be more different.

00:05:33.060 --> 00:05:36.660
Ali now owns his car free
and clear, and he can sell it

00:05:36.660 --> 00:05:39.750
for probably half to two
thirds of what he paid for it,

00:05:39.750 --> 00:05:41.790
or he can drive it until it falls apart,

00:05:41.790 --> 00:05:43.830
which could be five to 10 years from now,

00:05:43.830 --> 00:05:45.930
depending on how well he caress for it.

00:05:45.930 --> 00:05:47.160
- [Phillip] Michael, on the other hand,

00:05:47.160 --> 00:05:49.110
is right back where he started.

00:05:49.110 --> 00:05:51.990
Instead of buying out
the residual for $24,000,

00:05:51.990 --> 00:05:55.350
he opts to lease another new
car under the same terms.

00:05:55.350 --> 00:05:57.360
If we fast forward another three years,

00:05:57.360 --> 00:06:02.160
Michael will have paid
about $25,000 more than Ali.

00:06:02.160 --> 00:06:03.630
After a total of nine years,

00:06:03.630 --> 00:06:06.090
even if Ali has had to shell out $10,000

00:06:06.090 --> 00:06:07.620
in maintenance and repairs,

00:06:07.620 --> 00:06:11.550
he'll still be almost
$40,000 ahead of Michael.

00:06:11.550 --> 00:06:14.220
- Now, Michael isn't paying
that money for nothing.

00:06:14.220 --> 00:06:15.660
He gets to tool around town

00:06:15.660 --> 00:06:18.300
in a brand new ride
with all the amenities.

00:06:18.300 --> 00:06:21.930
Whether that's worth it
depends on what matters to you.

00:06:21.930 --> 00:06:24.210
But generally speaking,
if you can't afford

00:06:24.210 --> 00:06:26.700
to buy a new car, you
probably can't afford

00:06:26.700 --> 00:06:29.580
to drop $40,000 on a luxury, either.

00:06:29.580 --> 00:06:32.790
- At the end of the day, inflation
and rising interest rates

00:06:32.790 --> 00:06:35.040
haven't changed the math on leasing.

00:06:35.040 --> 00:06:37.230
- If you have a car in working order,

00:06:37.230 --> 00:06:40.950
take good care of it, and drive
it for as long as you can.

00:06:40.950 --> 00:06:43.170
And if you're in the market for a vehicle,

00:06:43.170 --> 00:06:45.180
buying, even with a car loan,

00:06:45.180 --> 00:06:47.490
still makes the most financial sense.

00:06:47.490 --> 00:06:49.740
- Leasing provides the short-term benefit

00:06:49.740 --> 00:06:52.500
of driving a nicer car for lower payments,

00:06:52.500 --> 00:06:56.191
but you're not solving a
problem, just buying time.

00:06:56.191 --> 00:06:58.680
(together) And that's our two cents.

00:06:58.680 --> 00:07:00.090
